{"authored" [{"question" "q-76114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2x+1 & =1 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+2x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "b725b9254b620db41e0c9912b9739f29b37cb1a62dc24142ac96508d902610f7" "basis" 1254 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-14770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+4x+10$ and the point $P(0,10)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(10,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-14770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,10).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+4x+10 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,10)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(10,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(10) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(10))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-14770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f3da035797dd8ede0d35ca851a12da73fe3f34dd7847c88da83bbe26d959c88"} {"question" "q-14770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-14770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-14770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=-2$ since the logarithm is not defined for negative numbers. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)+\\frac{2(x+2)}{2x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 0+1)+\\frac{2(0+2)}{2\\cdot 0+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =0+4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f3da035797dd8ede0d35ca851a12da73fe3f34dd7847c88da83bbe26d959c88"} {"question" "q-76112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2x+1 & =1 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+2x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f3da035797dd8ede0d35ca851a12da73fe3f34dd7847c88da83bbe26d959c88"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-110279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(5)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $5$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-110279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(5)=\\frac{g(5)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}+{g}^{′}(5){f}^{-1}(5).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=-1,$ $g(5)=-4,$ ${g}^{′}(5)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-1)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(5) & =\\frac{g(5)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}+{g}^{′}(5){f}^{-1}(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{{f}^{′}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{-5}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-110279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f3da035797dd8ede0d35ca851a12da73fe3f34dd7847c88da83bbe26d959c88"} {"question" "q-110279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-110279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-110279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-342584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-342584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-342584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9f3da035797dd8ede0d35ca851a12da73fe3f34dd7847c88da83bbe26d959c88"} {"question" "q-342584" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"} {"question" "q-342584" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043086/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d3b3ced8a8fdcf1814b1315395503bcb5b67604a68ba56480c616c851a1eac"}] "usage" {"q-76114" []} "basis" 1592 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186063700 17592186063701 17592186063702 17592186063703 17592186063704] "immutable_fields" [[17592186063699 :answer-field/correct] [17592186063699 :answer-field/id] [17592186063699 :answer-field/key] [17592186063699 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
